<BaseLayout title="Foot Acupressure Points" articleSchema={true} datePublished="2026-05-01" dateModified="2026-08-19" description="Discover foot acupressure points including KI1, LV3, and BL60. Learn how to stimulate these points for grounding, pain relief, and improved circulation." breadcrumbs={[{"label":"Home","href":"/"},{"label":"Body Atlas","href":"/atlas/"},{"label":"Foot","href":"/body/foot-acupressure-points/"}]}>
<article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
  <div class="text-center mb-10">
    <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-300">Body Region</span>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mt-2">Foot</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-2">A practical map of the foot's most useful acupressure points, from the grounding KI1 (Yongquan) to LV3 (Taichong). Foot points are easy to find, easy to reach barefoot, and the natural first step into reflexology-style self-care at home.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mb-10 flex justify-center"><svg viewBox="0 0 140 80" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-36 h-24"><path d="M20 20 Q30 8 70 8 Q110 8 120 20 Q130 35 125 55 Q120 70 100 70 L40 70 Q20 70 15 55 Q10 40 20 20 Z" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1.5"></path><circle cx="50" cy="40" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="70" cy="35" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="90" cy="40" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="60" cy="55" r="1.5" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.4"></circle><circle cx="80" cy="55" r="1.5" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.4"></circle><text x="70" y="78" font-family="system-ui" font-size="10" fill="#2d6a4f" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">Foot</text></svg></div>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Foot Acupressure Points Matter</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The feet carry the whole body all day, so many acupressure traditions treat the soles as a map of overall health. KI1 (Yongquan), at the center of the sole, is the most requested point in guides on grounding, insomnia, and stress because it is easy to reach. KD3 (Taixi), just behind the ankle, appears with it in routines focused on Kidney energy and fatigue, while BL60 (Kunlun) beside the ankle is a common stop for low back and leg discomfort. LV3 (Taichong), between the first two toes, rounds out the group as a classic for stress, headaches, and menstrual symptoms. The same points recur in our stress, sleep, headache, and low back guides, which is why the foot is an excellent starting region.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">From a meridian view, foot points sit along channels that run from the soles up the legs and into the trunk, which is how classical practice explains whole-body effects such as quieter sleep or less tense shoulders. Physically, slow, firm pressure on the sole also relaxes the fascia and muscles strained by hours of standing or walking. Both layers together make the foot a deeply satisfying region to press.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Key Points in This Region</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mb-5">Five points run from the sole to the ankle. All can be pressed with fingertips or a thumb, and most work well on both feet at once.</p>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <a href="/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point/" class="group flex items-center gap-4 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 transition-all hover:border-forest-400 hover:shadow-sm dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 dark:hover:border-forest-600">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-lg bg-forest-100 dark:bg-forest-900/40 flex items-center justify-center text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300 text-sm font-bold">KI1</div>
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">KI1 (Yongquan)<span class="text-xs text-stone-400 dark:text-stone-500 font-normal"> · Gushing Spring</span></h3>
