  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Foot Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Press the sole with your thumb or first knuckle at roughly a ninety-degree angle to the skin. Build pressure slowly over ten to fifteen seconds until you feel the deep, dull, spreading ache practitioners call deqi, then hold for one to two minutes with even breathing. Where a point sits near bone — BL60 beside the ankle, for example — ease off rather than pressing into the ridge. Press both feet, alternating as you go.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Does foot acupressure work like reflexology?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Foot acupressure and reflexology overlap a great deal: both stimulate zones on the sole and are often described in terms of a foot "map" of the body. Acupressure follows TCM meridian points, while reflexology uses its own zone system, but the hands-on feel is similar. Neither replaces medical treatment. A few minutes of slow pressure can feel pleasantly grounding, yet persistent pain or numbness should always be checked by a clinician.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Why does pressing KI1 help with sleep?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">KI1 (Yongquan) is the first point of the Kidney meridian, which TCM links to the deep reserves and calm yin energy that support restful sleep. It sits on the sole, about a third of the way from the toes to the heel. Slow, gentle pressure there before bed, combined with even breathing, can feel settling for many people. It is a relaxation tool, not a fix for insomnia — long-term sleep trouble deserves professional advice.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Foot points respond best to thirty seconds to two minutes of steady pressure, pressed on both feet. For a quick acute moment — tension after standing all day — thirty to sixty seconds can be enough. For deeper relaxation, move to the full one-to-two-minute hold and repeat on each sole. Let the deep, dull deqi sensation, not force, set the pace, and release slowly when finished.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 text-center">
    <a href="/atlas/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to Body Atlas</a>
  </div>

  <EvidenceBox refs={["lee-frazier-2011", "song-2015"]} note={"General evidence for acupressure from published systematic reviews. See condition-specific pages for targeted studies."} />
